      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Klawock%2C_Alaska",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1998 Klawock built a new Long House, Gaanì Ax Adi, complete with a fresh totem pole.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Klawock%2C_Alaska",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Alaska Native Brotherhood and Sisterhood, civil‑rights groups for Alaska Natives, were founded in Klawock in 1912.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Klawock%2C_Alaska",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Every February 16 Klawock hosts the Elizabeth Peratrovich Celebration, a potluck honoring her civil‑rights legacy.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://discoverpowisland.com/communities/klawock/",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Klawock was the site of Alaska’s first salmon cannery, opened in 1878 and shaping the town’s future.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.kupi.com/en/explore/united-states/klawock/history",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
